; The duplicate check is answered from an index now rather than by
; re-scanning `decls[0..i)` per declaration, and these are the four
; things that index has to keep getting right. Three of them are
; assertions about what IS reported; the fourth is an assertion about
; what is NOT, and it is the one a golden can make and a status cannot.
;
;   dup     three definitions of one name produce TWO diagnostics and
;           both point at the FIRST, not at the nearest. The index
;           appends in declaration order, so the first matching entry
;           in a bucket is the smallest index - that is where the rule
;           lives now;
;   Same    a `struct` and a `data` share the TYPE namespace, so this
;           collides;
;   mm      a `macro` occupies the VALUE namespace, so it collides
;           with a function - the arm `declNamespace` records;
;   Thing   a `data` and a `fn` of one spelling do NOT collide, and
;           there is deliberately no diagnostic about it below. A
;           bucket keyed on the name alone, without the namespace,
;           would report one here and this file would fail.
(fn (dup n) 1)
(fn (dup n) 2)
(fn (dup n) 3)

(data Same (MkSame Int))
(struct Same (x : Int))

(macro (mm x) (+ x 1))
(fn (mm n) 2)

(data Thing (MkThing Int))
(fn (Thing n) 1)

(:: main Int)
(fn (main) 0)
